# Project Overview

Healthcare costs are concentrated among a small percentage of patients. Identifying these individuals early enables healthcare providers and insurers to deliver targeted interventions, optimise resource allocation, and reduce long-term costs.

This project develops a predictive model using the **Medical Expenditure Panel Survey (MEPS)** to identify patients who are likely to remain in the highest healthcare spending group over a two-year period.

---

# Problem Statement

The objective of this project is to predict persistent high-cost patients using demographic characteristics, insurance information, chronic conditions, and prior healthcare expenditure.

---

# Dataset

**Source:** Medical Expenditure Panel Survey (MEPS)

The analysis uses longitudinal survey data containing:

* Demographic information
* Insurance coverage
* Chronic disease indicators
* Healthcare expenditure
* Survey weights

**Note:** The dataset is not included in this repository.

---

# Methodology

The project follows these steps:

* Data cleaning
* Feature engineering
* Survey-weight adjustment
* Logistic Regression modelling
* Model evaluation
* Risk stratification

---

# Model Performance

| Metric      |     Score |
| ----------- | --------: |
| AUC         | **0.934** |
| Accuracy    | **81.9%** |
| Sensitivity | **90.1%** |
| Specificity | **80.8%** |

---

# Key Findings

* Prior healthcare expenditure was the strongest predictor.
* Patients in the highest predicted-risk group had significantly higher healthcare costs.
* The model can support targeted care management and healthcare resource allocation.

